WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.098
Picture a café at the morning rush, with one barista.

2
00:00:06.098 --> 00:00:10.201
She takes an order, starts the milk, then stands and watches it.

3
00:00:10.433 --> 00:00:13.896
The line grows, and the only thing working is the machine.

4
00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:16.895
The café's payments program does the same.

5
00:00:16.895 --> 00:00:21.638
Each payment waits about 120 milliseconds for the card's bank to say yes.

6
00:00:21.638 --> 00:00:24.107
A thread that waits does nothing else.

7
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.746
The easy fix is one barista per customer: one thread per payment.

8
00:00:28.746 --> 00:00:30.953
In Java that's a platform thread.

9
00:00:30.953 --> 00:00:36.303
Each one is a task the kernel counts, with about 2 megabytes of stack set aside.

10
00:00:36.533 --> 00:00:38.884
Now 10,000 payments wait at once.

11
00:00:38.884 --> 00:00:45.293
That's 10,000 tasks and about 20 gigabytes of stack addresses, mostly to watch milk steam.

12
00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:50.630
In the lab, the program ran in a container that allows 64 tasks.

13
00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:51.896
Watch the count.

14
00:00:51.896 --> 00:00:55.851
The shell and the JVM's own threads take about 22.

15
00:00:55.851 --> 00:00:57.433
The payments get 42.

16
00:00:57.933 --> 00:01:01.364
The next one fails, with an error that blames memory.

17
00:01:01.364 --> 00:01:03.176
But memory sat nearly empty.

18
00:01:03.176 --> 00:01:05.377
The kernel had refused a new task.

19
00:01:05.733 --> 00:01:11.628
One way out is never to wait: hand over a function to run when the answer comes, and move on.

20
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:19.629
It works, but the code turns into chains of callbacks, and when something fails, the error no longer shows the path that led there.

21
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:25.581
What we want is plain code that waits, top to bottom, without a kernel task for every wait.

22
00:01:25.867 --> 00:01:27.028
Back to the café.

23
00:01:27.028 --> 00:01:30.787
Keep two baristas, and give them a rail of order slips.

24
00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:33.686
A barista takes a slip and starts the milk.

25
00:01:33.686 --> 00:01:39.239
Then, instead of watching it, she pins the slip up by the machine, and takes the next one.

26
00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:45.148
When the milk is ready, that slip goes back in line, and whichever barista is free picks it up.

27
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:45.853
Map it.

28
00:01:45.853 --> 00:01:49.870
The baristas are a few real threads, the kind the kernel runs.

29
00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:51.619
The slips are the payments.

30
00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:54.664
And the steaming milk is the wait for the bank.

31
00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:57.727
Java has worked this way since JDK 21.

32
00:01:57.727 --> 00:02:00.512
The slips are called virtual threads.

33
00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:04.699
A virtual thread is a Java object, not a kernel task.

34
00:02:04.699 --> 00:02:13.751
To run, it mounts on a carrier: an ordinary platform thread from a small pool the JVM keeps, by default one per processor.

35
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:14.692
Watch V2.

36
00:02:14.692 --> 00:02:16.846
It asks the bank, and waits.

37
00:02:16.846 --> 00:02:24.537
The JVM unmounts it: it saves V2's stack, its place in the code, on the heap, and frees the carrier.

38
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.916
worker-2 picks up V3 at once.

39
00:02:26.916 --> 00:02:31.348
The kernel noticed nothing: task 67 never stopped running.

40
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:37.807
When the bank answers, V2 goes back on the rail, and mounts on whichever carrier is free first.

41
00:02:37.807 --> 00:02:39.571
This time, that's worker-1.

42
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:48.620
Now the lab again: the same container, the same limit of 64 tasks, and 10,000 payments, each on its own virtual thread.

43
00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.185
How many tasks does the kernel see?

44
00:02:51.185 --> 00:02:52.357
Pause and guess.

45
00:02:55.567 --> 00:02:55.817
37.

46
00:02:55.817 --> 00:02:58.991
All 10,000 started and waited at once.

47
00:02:58.991 --> 00:03:06.509
The kernel saw the JVM's usual helpers, plus one carrier per processor, 12 in the lab box.

48
00:03:06.767 --> 00:03:09.154
It had no idea it was running a café.

49
00:03:09.154 --> 00:03:11.089
The waiting lived on the heap.

50
00:03:11.567 --> 00:03:13.432
Here the café picture breaks.

51
00:03:13.432 --> 00:03:15.297
An order slip is a few words.

52
00:03:15.297 --> 00:03:19.928
A virtual thread's slip is its whole stack, copied to the heap and back.

53
00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:23.150
And a few waits still hold on to the carrier.

54
00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:24.479
That's called pinning.

55
00:03:24.733 --> 00:03:33.679
In JDK 25, waiting inside a class's static initializer, code that runs once when a class is first used, still pins.

56
00:03:33.933 --> 00:03:42.153
If every carrier gets pinned, no other virtual thread in the JVM can run, like baristas each stuck holding one cup.

57
00:03:42.533 --> 00:03:44.766
And virtual threads aren't faster.

58
00:03:44.766 --> 00:03:49.757
Java's own proposal says it plainly: Virtual threads are not faster threads.

59
00:03:50.033 --> 00:03:52.341
They let far more tasks wait at once.

60
00:03:52.341 --> 00:03:57.145
So they suit a program that spends its time waiting, on a bank or a database.

61
00:03:57.145 --> 00:03:59.952
A thread that's busy computing gains nothing.

62
00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:03.279
And make a new one per job; don't keep them in a pool.

63
00:04:03.667 --> 00:04:06.821
So how does one program wait on 10,000 payments?

64
00:04:06.821 --> 00:04:09.647
It stops giving every wait its own barista.

65
00:04:09.647 --> 00:04:15.889
The waits sit on the rail, a few baristas do the work, and your code still reads top to bottom.

66
00:04:16.333 --> 00:04:17.448
One quick check.

67
00:04:17.448 --> 00:04:19.399
The lab box has 12 carriers.

68
00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:23.649
Twelve virtual threads each wait inside a static initializer.

69
00:04:23.649 --> 00:04:27.689
What happens to the next virtual thread that wants to run?

70
00:04:27.689 --> 00:04:28.804
Pause and think.

71
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:32.624
It waits.

72
00:04:32.624 --> 00:04:37.475
Every carrier is pinned, so nothing else can mount until one frees up.
